# death_if_fails rescaled for real per-act risk (drafts/repeats/rescale_death.py)
# Chroma library, modern Earth: EVERYDAY CRIME AND ITS CONSEQUENCES (next batch, Emren's point 11: "a world of
# possibilities with color philosophy framework"). Brief: drafts/next2/BRIEF.md. Syntax: header of build.py.
# No moment here takes a life and no option carries kills: (the Library thread writes those in earth-crime-life.lib).
# Twelve moments: ten everyday moments whose calls hold each of the ten color pairs once (every color is called four
# times, and the pairs are placed so each stage is called as evenly as its few moments allow), one life event (pairs
# D5b) and one echo. Cross cycles: s1 three times, s2 three, s3 three, s4 twice.
# Wrongs are pickable in every color, and so are refusing, walking away, owning up and calling for help.
# Rates for the life event: sources/life-events-and-everyday.md Part F1 (physical fights by stage) and D74 (assault).

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- juvenile: a shoplifting dare (calls BR, cycle s3)

== a dare to steal from the shop
tier: everyday
stages: juvenile
age: 13-16
calls: BR
tone: trouble
life: friends, money
horizon: moment
roles: friend, sibling, a shopkeeper
earth: your friends dare you to walk out of the chemist with something you have not paid for
times: About 1 in 3 young teenagers, once or twice (estimate); 1 in 5 US high-schoolers stole from a shop in the past year (Josephson Institute 2012)
gap: 2-4
share: .33
rate: 1
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: In the chemist on the high street {friend} nods at the rack of earphones and grins. Everyone else has done it; now it is {Ns} turn.
scene W: {N} knows the woman at the till by name. She asked after {Ns} family last week, and now the others are waiting by the door to see whether {N} will rob her.
scene U: {N} has already counted the cameras: two, and one of them points at the wrong aisle. Knowing that is not the same as wanting to use it.
scene B: The earphones cost more than a month's pocket money. Nobody would miss one pair, and the others would stop calling {N} the baby of the group.
scene R: {Ns} heart is thumping so hard it is nearly a laugh. The door is ten steps away, and everyone is watching.
scene G: These are the friends {N} grew up with, the same three streets and the same school. Saying no could mean walking home alone.
- W | tell the woman at the till what your friends are planning | v: conformity, security | closed: approval: telling on friends; backfire: frozen out by the whole group for months | mark: made an enemy | chance: 70
- U | say no, and point out the camera above the door to the others | v: security | self_control: + | chance: 70
- B | pocket a pair of earphones while the others watch | habit | v: power, achievement | closed: law: shoplifting; backfire: stopped at the door, the police called and a parent fetched | mark: broke the law | chance: 70
- R | grab a chocolate bar, laugh, and bolt for the door | body: light | v: stimulation | closed: law: shoplifting; backfire: chased down the street and marched back in | mark: took a wild risk | self_control: - | chance: 65
- G | say your gran would never get over it, and wait outside | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 80
- W>R | refuse, and dare them all to jump off the high board at the pool | v: stimulation, conformity | chance: 60
- U>G | find a way to say no that keeps you all friends | v: benevolence, security | act: find a way to say no that keeps them all friends | chance: 60
- B>W | tell them to put it all back, or you go to the manager | v: power, conformity | act: tell them to put it all back, or they will go to the manager | chance: 60
- R>U | ask the oldest one straight out why everyone has to do it | v: self-direction | chance: 65
- G>B | let the others take the risk, and share in what they bring out | v: power (resources), benevolence | closed: law: handling stolen goods; backfire: the whole group banned from the shop, and parents called | mark: gave in to pressure | chance: 50
works: {N} walks out into the afternoon with the others, and nothing comes after.
works: By the bus stop the dare has turned into a joke, and {N} is still one of the group.
fails: A hand lands on a shoulder at the door, and the manager rings home.
fails: The others go cold on {N}, and the story goes round the whole year.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- juvenile: a friend's bag (calls UG, cycle s1)

== a bag you should not ask about
tier: everyday
at: police
stages: juvenile
age: 13-17
calls: UG
tone: trouble
life: friends, home
horizon: week
roles: friend, parent, grandparent, the police
earth: a friend asks you to keep a zipped bag at your house for a few days, and not to look inside
times: Uncommon: perhaps 1 in 10 teenagers, once (estimate)
once: yes
share: .1
rate: .5
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: {friend} turns up at the back door with a zipped sports bag and asks {N} to keep it under the bed for a few days. Do not open it, {friend} says. Do not ask.
scene W: There are rules in this house, and a bag nobody may open breaks most of them. {N} has never hidden anything this big from the family.
scene U: The bag is heavier than clothes would be, and {friend} keeps glancing back at the street. {N} wants to know what is inside before deciding anything.
scene B: {friend} says there will be a little cash in it for {N} at the end of the week. {N} weighs the bag in one hand and the offer in the other.
scene R: {friend} looks scared, properly scared, for the first time {N} can remember. Saying no would feel like shutting the door in that face.
scene G: {friend} and {N} have been in and out of each other's houses since they were five. That counts for something, bag or no bag.
- W | take the bag straight to the police station yourself | v: conformity, security | closed: approval: handing in a friend; backfire: the friend is questioned, and the whole school knows who did it | mark: made an enemy | act: take the bag straight to the police station themselves | chance: 70
- U | unzip it once they have gone, and see for yourself | v: self-direction | closed: approval: going behind a friend's back; backfire: the friend spots the zip, and the trust is gone | mark: hid a wrong | act: unzip it once the friend has gone, and see for themselves | chance: 80
- B | say no: a little cash is not worth that kind of risk | v: security, power | chance: 85
- R | hand it back on the doorstep and say they are out of their mind | v: self-direction, benevolence | chance: 85
- G | hide it in your grandad's allotment shed, where nobody ever looks | v: benevolence, security | closed: law: keeping goods that may be stolen; backfire: the shed is searched, and the family dragged in | mark: broke the law | chance: 75
- W>U | say it stays only if they tell you exactly what is in it | v: security | closed: law: keeping goods that may be stolen; backfire: the police knock, and knowing what was inside makes it worse | act: say it stays only if the friend says exactly what is in it | chance: 70
- U>B | find out what is inside, and what it is worth to them | v: power | chance: 60
- B>R | keep it, if they lend you their new bike for the week | v: hedonism, power | closed: law: keeping goods that may be stolen; backfire: the police knock, and the bag is in the wardrobe | mark: broke the law | act: keep it, if the friend lends them that new bike for the week | chance: 55
- R>G | tell them you are scared for them, and to get rid of it | v: benevolence | act: tell the friend they are scared for them, and to get rid of it | chance: 55
- G>W | ask your friend to sit down with your mum and tell her everything | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 65
works: The bag is gone by Friday, and nobody ever mentions it again.
works: {friend} comes back with a long, honest story, and the two of them sort it out together.
fails: Two police officers knock at the door on Thursday evening, asking about a bag.
fails: {friend} stops speaking to {N}, and the whole school hears a version of it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- life event: a fall after a shove (pairs D5b)
# The stranger is hurt, never killed: the worst outcome is a week in hospital.

== a stranger on the pavement who does not get up
tier: life event
stages: young_adult adult
age: 17-40
per_year: 0 0 .01 .003 0 0
drivers: harsh+.3 unrest+.3 money-.3 family-.2 stress+.2 trouble+.15
gap: 3-10
times: rare: about 1 in 100 young adults and 3 in 1,000 adults a year are in a fight where someone goes down hard and does not get up at once, out of about 6 in 100 young adults and 2 in 100 adults in any fight a year (Part F1; the share is an estimate); men far more often than women
likelier: youth, being a man, drinking, late nights in town, friends who fight, money trouble, a fight before
rarer: a partner and children, steady work, a quiet town, not drinking, age
tone: trouble
life: conflict, body, friends
horizon: months
roles: friend, parent, partner, the police, a paramedic, strangers
earth: a shove outside a takeaway after midnight, you shove back, and the other man falls and does not get up
scene: Outside the takeaway after midnight there is a shout and a shove, and {N} shoves back. The stranger stumbles off the kerb, goes down hard, and does not get up.
scene W: {N} pushed him. Nobody else did. Whatever comes next, {N} is the one who has to answer for it, and {Ns} phone is already out.
scene U: {N} sees everything with a strange, slow clarity: the camera over the takeaway door, the stranger's friend running up, the stranger's chest rising and falling.
scene B: In a few minutes there will be police here asking who pushed whom. Only {friend} saw it properly. {N} starts thinking very fast.
scene R: It happened in a second. {Ns} hands are shaking, the blood is roaring, and the stranger's friend is coming straight at {N}, shouting.
scene G: The stranger is somebody's son, lying very still on the cold pavement. The whole street seems to go quiet around him.
- W | wait by him for the police, and tell them the push was yours | binds | v: conformity, benevolence | mark: owned up | title_if_fails: someone with a record | act: wait by him for the police, and tell them the push was theirs | chance: 56
- U | tell the police he slipped, and leave the push out of it | v: security | closed: law: lying to the police; backfire: the footage shows the push, and the lie makes it worse | mark: hid a wrong | title_if_fails: someone with a record | chance: 60
- B | walk away fast, before the camera over the door gets a good look | v: security | closed: law: leaving the scene of an assault; backfire: picked out on the footage and charged | mark: broke the law | title_if_fails: someone with a record | chance: 72
- R | run into the takeaway shouting for someone to ring an ambulance | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 77
- G | send your friend home, so they are not caught up in it | v: benevolence | chance: 75
- WB | give a full statement that makes clear he shoved first | v: security, conformity | chance: 70
- WU | ring for an ambulance and do exactly what the call handler says | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 90
- UG | kneel by him, keep him warm, and keep talking to him until help comes | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 62
- BR | film the whole scene on your phone, loudly, so nobody can twist it later | v: security | tech: phone | chance: 58
- RG | stand between him and his furious friend until the paramedics come | body: heavy | v: benevolence, security | chance: 60
works: The stranger sits up before the ambulance arrives, dazed and swearing, and is home by morning.
works: The police take statements, the footage shows a shove and a fall, and nothing more comes of it.
fails: The stranger spends a week in hospital, and the police want to talk to {N} again.
fails: {N} is arrested at dawn, and the charge is assault.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- young adult: selling a little weed (calls WB, cycle s2)

== selling a little weed for quick money
tier: everyday
at: police
stages: young_adult
age: 18-29
calls: WB
tone: mixed
life: money, friends
horizon: months
roles: friend, parent, the police
earth: a friend with more weed than they can smoke offers you a share to sell on to people you know
times: Uncommon: perhaps 1 in 10 young adults are offered the chance, once or twice (estimate)
gap: 3-8
share: .1
rate: .5
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: {friend} has more weed than one person could smoke in a year, and a ready market among the people {N} knows. Easy money, {friend} says, and nobody gets hurt.
scene W: It is against the law, whatever everyone at the party thinks, and {N} has just applied for a job that asks about convictions.
scene U: {N} works it through: how many friends would buy, what it would bring in, and how few people it takes for word to reach the wrong ears.
scene B: The rent is due in nine days and {Ns} account is nearly empty. {friend}'s offer would cover it twice over.
scene R: There is something glamorous about it, {N} has to admit: being the one everyone texts on a Friday night.
scene G: It is only a plant, {N} thinks, and half the street grows something on a windowsill. Still, selling it would change how people look at {N}.
- W | warn your friend you will report them if they ask anyone else | v: conformity, security | closed: approval: threatening a friend; backfire: the friend tells everyone who would grass on them | mark: made an enemy | chance: 80
- U | work out what a conviction would cost you, and turn it down | v: security | mark: turned down a chance | act: work out what a conviction would cost them, and turn it down | chance: 85
- B | turn it down, and ask your friend for a loan instead, at no interest | v: power (resources), security | chance: 70
- R | say yes for the summer, and enjoy being the one everyone texts | habit | v: hedonism, stimulation | closed: law: supplying drugs; backfire: a buyer talks, and the police come | mark: took a wild risk | law: drugs | chance: 80
- G | pass a little on to old friends at cost, and never to strangers | v: benevolence, tradition | closed: law: supplying drugs, even at cost; backfire: one old friend's flat is raided, and the trail leads back | mark: broke the law | law: drugs | chance: 80
- W>B | take extra shifts on the books until the rent is covered | v: achievement, security | chance: 60
- U>R | sell a little at a festival, where nobody knows your face | v: hedonism | closed: law: supplying drugs; backfire: stopped by security at the gate, and the police called | mark: broke the law | law: drugs | chance: 55
- B>G | sell just enough to clear your mum's gas bill, then stop | v: benevolence, security | closed: law: supplying drugs; backfire: one sale too many, and the police at the family door | mark: broke the law | law: drugs | chance: 70
- R>W | tell your friend straight that this will get them locked up | v: benevolence, security | chance: 60
- G>U | wait a month, and watch what happens to the friends who do sell | v: security, self-direction | chance: 60
works: The rent gets paid one way or another, and nobody comes knocking.
works: By autumn it is all behind {N}, and {friend} is still a friend.
fails: A police car pulls up outside the flat one evening.
fails: Word gets round, and {N} is suddenly the one other people's parents warn them about.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- young adult and adult: a scam that pays (calls UR, cycle s4)

== a friend's scam is paying well
tier: everyday
at: police
stages: young_adult adult
age: 18-40
calls: UR
tone: mixed
life: money, friends
horizon: months
roles: friend, parent, the police, strangers online
earth: a friend is making good money selling festival tickets that do not exist, and offers you half to help
times: Uncommon: perhaps 1 in 15 young adults, once or twice (estimate)
gap: 6-12
share: .067
rate: .5
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: {friend} turns the laptop round: nine hundred this week, from strangers buying festival tickets that do not exist. Half of it could be {Ns}, {friend} says, for running a few of the listings.
scene W: At the other end of every sale is someone who saved up for a weekend that will never happen. {N} keeps seeing the person, not the money.
scene U: {N} sees exactly how it works, and exactly where it breaks: the bank that freezes an account, the one angry buyer who goes to the police.
scene B: Nine hundred a week. {N} has done everything properly for years and has less to show for it than {friend} has after a month.
scene R: {friend} is laughing and flush and buying every round. The money is fast and loud, and part of {N} wants some of it.
scene G: {N} and {friend} go back to primary school. {N} does not want the money, and does not want to lose {friend} to this either.
- W | post a warning about the fake listings, with your friend's name on it | identity | v: universalism, conformity | closed: approval: shaming a friend in public; backfire: the post spreads, and the friend's family turns on the poster | mark: made an enemy | tech: internet | chance: 75
- U | run the listings for a month, then get out before it unravels | v: achievement, power | closed: law: fraud; backfire: the bank freezes the account and passes the name to the police | mark: broke the law | title_if_fails: someone with a record | tech: internet | chance: 70
- B | turn it down, and start reselling real tickets at a fair markup | identity | v: achievement, power | chance: 60
- R | tell your friend it is disgusting, and walk out of the flat | identity | v: universalism, self-direction | chance: 75
- G | take a share for the family, and never ask where it came from | binds | v: benevolence, security | closed: law: handling the proceeds of fraud; backfire: the police trace the money to the family's account | mark: hid a wrong | chance: 70
- W>G | sit your friend down with their family, so the family can talk sense into them | v: benevolence, security | chance: 55
- U>W | save every listing, and send them all to the police without a name | v: universalism, security | chance: 60
- B>U | ask to see how the money moves, and keep a copy, just in case | v: power, security | chance: 70
- R>B | say yes on the spot, and spend the first share that night | v: hedonism, power | closed: law: fraud; backfire: arrested when a buyer goes to the police | mark: broke the law | chance: 55
- G>R | let it be: your friend lives how they like, and so do you | v: self-direction | chance: 60
works: Whatever {N} chose, nothing comes back to {N}, and the summer goes on.
works: By the end of the summer the listings are gone, and {friend} is looking for real work.
fails: A letter from the bank says {Ns} account is frozen while the police make enquiries.
fails: {friend} hears what {N} did, and a friendship of twenty years ends in one phone call.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- adult: a pub argument that turns (calls RG, cycle s1)

== a pub argument turns into a fight
tier: everyday
stages: adult
age: 25-50
calls: RG
tone: trouble
life: friends, conflict, body
horizon: moment
roles: friend, a barman, strangers
earth: an argument at the bar turns, and a man shoves your friend into the tables
times: Perhaps 1 in 3 adults who drink in pubs, once or twice (estimate); men far more often than women
gap: 4-8
share: .33
rate: 1
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: Somewhere between the third pint and the fourth, an argument about nothing turns. A man at the bar shoves {friend} into the tables, the glasses go over, and the whole pub goes quiet.
scene W: Even a pub on a Friday night has its rules, and the barman is already reaching for the phone. Someone needs to calm this before it becomes a matter for the police.
scene U: {N} sees how it will go: the man is bigger and drunker than {friend}, his mate is circling behind, and the door is twelve steps away.
scene B: {N} has a lot to lose: the job, a clean record, the mortgage application going in next week. Whatever happens in the next ten seconds must not land on {N}.
scene R: The shove lands on {friend}, and {N} feels it like a shove of {Ns} own. {Ns} fists are tight before any thought arrives.
scene G: {friend} has stood by {N} for twenty years. Nobody puts hands on {friend} while {N} is standing there.
- W | step between them with your hands up, and tell them both to stop | body: heavy | v: security, benevolence | death_if_fails: 0.001 | chance: 65
- U | catch your friend's eye, nod at the door, and leave together | v: security | chance: 65
- B | keep out of it, and make sure the barman sees you had no part | v: security, power | mark: refused someone in need | self_control: + | chance: 75
- R | hit him, hard, before he can swing again | body: heavy | v: power, security | closed: law: assault; backfire: arrested and charged | mark: hurt someone badly | death_if_fails: 0.001 | title_if_fails: someone with a record | role: men | chance: 50
- G | wade in and drag your friend out by the collar | body: heavy | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | death_if_fails: 0.001 | chance: 55
- W>U | sit them both down, and make each one say what started it | v: security | chance: 50
- U>B | wait for your moment, then shove him off balance and get out | body: heavy | v: power, security | closed: law: assault; backfire: he falls badly, and the cameras saw it all | mark: broke the law | death_if_fails: 0.001 | chance: 50
- B>R | get his mate first, so it is two on one | body: heavy | v: power, stimulation | closed: law: affray; backfire: both arrested, and the night on the local news | mark: hurt someone badly | death_if_fails: 0.001 | role: men | chance: 40
- R>G | roar at him to back off, loud enough to stop the whole pub | v: benevolence | chance: 65
- G>W | call the regulars over, and let the room calm him down | v: security, tradition | chance: 60
works: The man is steered out into the night, and the pub goes back to its noise.
works: {N} and {friend} walk home under the streetlights, sore and laughing.
fails: There is a brawl, a broken table, and a police van outside within ten minutes.
fails: {N} wakes up with a split lip and a statement to give at the station.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- adult: fiddling the books (calls WU, cycle s2)

== fiddling the books at work
tier: everyday
at: employer
stages: adult
age: 25-50
calls: WU
needs: career
tone: mixed
life: work, money
horizon: months
roles: boss, colleague, partner
earth: the expense claims at work go through you, and you notice that nobody ever checks them
times: Uncommon: some office workers who handle claims, once or twice in a working life (estimate); expense schemes are about 1 in 10 workplace frauds (ACFE)
gap: 6-12
share: .15
rate: .5
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: The expense claims go through {N} now, and {N} has noticed what nobody else seems to have: nobody checks them. A hundred here and two hundred there, and the books would still balance.
scene W: {boss} gave {N} the books because {N} was the honest one. That trust sits on the desk like a paperweight.
scene U: {N} can see the gap in the system as clearly as a missing tooth: one signature, never checked, and an audit only every three years.
scene B: {N} has given this firm years of unpaid overtime. A few hundred a month would only be taking back what is owed.
scene R: The pay is poor and the days are long, and for one giddy afternoon the thought of getting away with something is the most alive {N} has felt in years.
scene G: Times are tight at home, and the bills come faster than the pay. The firm would never feel it; the household would.
- W | go back through every colleague's old claims and report each slip | v: conformity | closed: approval: turning on colleagues; backfire: the office freezes the reporter out, and a friend loses their job | mark: made an enemy | chance: 70
- U | put through one small claim, just to see whether anyone notices | v: self-direction | closed: law: false accounting; backfire: flagged at the audit, and every claim checked | mark: hid a wrong | self_control: - | chance: 75
- B | take the gap to the boss, and ask for a raise for finding it | v: achievement, power | chance: 60
- R | hand in your notice, and go and find work that does not bore you | v: self-direction, stimulation | act: hand in their notice, and go and find work that does not bore them | chance: 55
- G | take home the spare office supplies, as everyone always has | v: tradition | closed: law: theft from an employer; backfire: a new manager counts the stock, and a warning goes on file | mark: hid a wrong | chance: 70
- W>B | ask, through the proper channels, for the overtime you are owed | v: achievement, conformity | chance: 50
- U>R | use what you know of the books to go freelance, and be your own boss | identity | v: self-direction | chance: 45
- B>G | take just enough to cover the bills at home, and mean to pay it back | v: benevolence, security | closed: law: theft from an employer; backfire: found at the year-end, and the job gone | mark: broke the law | title_if_fails: someone with a record | self_control: - | chance: 60
- R>W | blurt out in the meeting that nobody ever checks the claims | v: universalism, conformity | mark: defied an authority | chance: 65
- G>U | sleep on it for a week, and see whether the urge passes | v: self-direction | self_control: + | chance: 50
works: The year-end comes and goes, and {N} sleeps well.
works: A month later things at work have changed, and {N} is glad of the change.
fails: The auditors arrive early, and they ask for {N} by name.
fails: {boss} calls {N} in, closes the door, and puts a file on the desk.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- adult and mature: a neighbour's van (calls WR, cycle s3)

== the neighbour's van across your drive
tier: everyday
stages: adult mature
