# ---------------------------------------------------------------- mature and elder: an insurance claim (calls UB, cycle s1)

== an insurance claim for more than you lost
tier: everyday
stages: mature elder
age: 40-85
calls: UB
tone: mixed
life: money, home
horizon: months
roles: partner, sibling, a loss adjuster, neighbours
earth: after a break-in the insurance form asks what was taken, and nobody would know if the list were longer
times: Uncommon: perhaps 1 in 5 people claim for a break-in in later life, and some are tempted to pad it (estimate); about 13 in 1,000 US households are burgled a year (BJS 2023)
gap: 8-15
share: .2
rate: .5
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: The burglars took a laptop and a little jewellery. The insurance form has twelve empty lines, and the loss adjuster said on the phone that receipts help but are not essential.
scene W: {N} has paid the premium every month for twenty-six years without a single claim. The form asks for the truth and nothing more, and there is a box to sign at the bottom.
scene U: {N} reads the policy twice and knows exactly what it covers, what it does not, and how the company checks.
scene B: Twenty-six years of premiums, and now the company will try to pay as little as it can. {N} can see who comes out ahead if nobody pushes back.
scene R: {N} feels sick every time they walk into the bedroom where the drawers were tipped out. Somebody owes {N} for that feeling, and the form is all there is.
scene G: One of the rings was {Ns} grandmother's. No form has a line for what that was worth.
- W | list exactly what was taken, nothing more, and sign it | v: conformity | chance: 70
- U | read the policy line by line, and claim every penny it truly covers | v: security, achievement | chance: 75
- B | haggle with the adjuster over every item until the offer is fair | v: power, achievement | chance: 70
- R | ring the insurer and tell them exactly what this has done to you | v: self-direction | act: ring the insurer and tell them exactly what this has done to them | chance: 65
- G | claim little, and let the things go; the ring was never about money | v: tradition (accepting what is) | chance: 75
- W>U | photograph and list everything you own from now on, room by room | habit | v: security | chance: 65
- U>B | add a watch that nobody could ever prove you did not own | v: power (resources) | closed: law: insurance fraud; backfire: the adjuster checks, the claim is refused, and the police are told | mark: broke the law | title_if_fails: someone with a record | act: add a watch that nobody could ever prove they did not own | chance: 60
- B>R | push the adjuster for a fast payout, and spend it on a holiday | v: hedonism, power | chance: 65
- R>G | value the ring at what it meant to you, not what it cost | v: tradition | closed: law: an inflated claim; backfire: the valuation is checked, and the whole claim stalls | mark: hid a wrong | act: value the ring at what it meant to them, not what it cost | chance: 70
- G>W | claim the way the neighbours did, with a little extra, as everyone does | v: conformity, tradition | self_control: - | closed: law: insurance fraud; backfire: the insurer looks hard at the whole street's claims | mark: gave in to pressure | title_if_fails: someone with a record | chance: 60
works: The payout arrives within the month, and the matter is closed.
works: {N} buys a new lock and a new laptop, and the house starts to feel like home again.
fails: The insurer sends an investigator, who asks a great many questions.
fails: The claim drags on for half a year, and the payout is a fraction of the loss.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- holders of a new record: a night in a cell (calls WG, cycle s4)

== the first night in a cell
tier: everyday
at: police
rate: .5
stages: juvenile+
age: 16-85
calls: WG
holds: someone with a record
tenure: 0-1
once: yes
tone: trouble
life: conflict, body
horizon: moment
roles: parent, a custody officer, a solicitor
earth: a steel door shuts, the hatch slides closed, and the first night in a cell begins
times: Once, on the first night after an arrest that brings a record; 3 to 4 in 10 Americans are arrested by 23, mostly for minor things (Brame 2012), far fewer held overnight
scene: The door shuts with a sound {N} will remember for years. A thin mattress, a hatch in the door, a light that never goes off, and a whole night ahead.
scene W: {N} broke the law, and the law has answered. There is a strange order to it: the forms, the times, the officer who checks the hatch every hour.
scene U: {N} lies on the mattress and goes over it again and again: every choice that led here, and which one, if any, could have gone the other way.
scene B: {N} is already thinking about tomorrow: the solicitor, the story, who knows what, and how to come out of this with as little lost as possible.
scene R: The walls are too close. {N} paces, three steps and turn, three steps and turn, with a shout sitting just under the ribs.
scene G: Somewhere out there the family will have heard by now. {N} thinks of the kitchen at home and the light left on over the back door.
- W | ask the officer for the rules, and keep every one of them | v: conformity, security | chance: 75
- U | go over the whole thing, step by step, and find where it turned | v: self-direction | chance: 75
- B | work out what to say tomorrow that gets you home soonest | v: power, security | act: work out what to say tomorrow that gets them home soonest | chance: 75
- R | kick the door and shout until someone comes | body: light | v: self-direction | closed: approval: kicking off in custody; backfire: a longer stay, and another charge | mark: defied an authority | chance: 45
- G | lie still, breathe slowly, and let the night pass the way nights do | v: tradition (accepting what is), security | chance: 70
- W>G | ask for the phone call you are allowed, and ring home | v: benevolence, conformity | act: ask for the phone call they are allowed, and ring home | chance: 65
- U>W | ask for the duty solicitor, and learn exactly what you face | v: security, conformity | chance: 65
- B>U | chat to the officer at the hatch, and find out what they know | v: security, power | chance: 65
- R>B | swear to yourself that nobody will ever lock you up again | v: power, self-direction | act: swear to themselves that nobody will ever lock them up again | chance: 95
- G>R | hum the songs from home under your breath, to keep yourself together | habit | v: tradition | act: hum the songs from home under their breath, to keep themselves together | chance: 70
works: Morning comes, and {N} walks out into the daylight steadier than when the door shut.
works: The night passes, and {N} even sleeps a little before the hatch opens on breakfast.
fails: The night is endless, and by morning {N} is shaking with tiredness.
fails: The night goes badly, and {N} comes out with more trouble than before.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- adult and mature: an alibi (calls BG, cycle s2)

== a friend asks you for an alibi
tier: everyday
at: police
stages: adult mature
age: 25-65
calls: BG
tone: trouble
life: friends, conflict
horizon: week
roles: friend, partner, the police
earth: the police are asking where your friend was on Saturday night, and your friend asks you to say they were with you
times: Rare: perhaps 1 in 20 adults, once (estimate)
once: yes
share: .05
rate: .5
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: {friend} rings late, voice low. The police are asking about Saturday night and a smashed shop window on the high street, and {friend} wants {N} to say they were together all evening.
scene W: Lying to the police is a crime of its own, {N} knows, and it would make {N} part of whatever {friend} did.
scene U: {N} thinks it through: who else saw {friend} on Saturday, which phones were where, how many places a story like that can come apart.
scene B: {friend} would owe {N} for this, properly owe, for years. That is worth something. So is staying out of it.
scene R: {N} hears the fear in {friend}'s voice and wants to say yes before the sentence is finished.
scene G: {friend} has been family to {N} for thirty years: weddings, funerals, the bad winter. Families stand together; that is what {N} was always taught.
- W | ring the police yourself, before your friend can ask anyone else | v: conformity, security | closed: approval: turning in a friend; backfire: the friend's family never speaks to the caller again | mark: made an enemy | act: ring the police themselves, before their friend can ask anyone else | chance: 75
- U | say nothing either way yet, and keep every message they send | v: security | chance: 80
- B | say yes, and make sure they know they owe you for life | binds | v: power | closed: law: giving the police a false alibi; backfire: the story comes apart, and both are charged | mark: broke the law | title_if_fails: someone with a record | act: say yes, and make sure the friend knows they owe them for life | chance: 60
- R | tell your friend straight to face it, and that you will go with them | binds | v: benevolence | self_control: + | chance: 70
- G | say yes: you were together, as far as anyone needs to know | identity | v: benevolence, tradition | closed: law: giving the police a false alibi; backfire: a camera on the high street says otherwise, and both are charged | mark: hid a wrong | title_if_fails: someone with a record | chance: 60
- W>B | tell the police the truth, and ask them to keep your name out of it | v: security, conformity | chance: 55
- U>R | help your friend put together a story that does not need you in it | v: benevolence | closed: law: helping to mislead the police; backfire: the story falls apart in the interview room | mark: hid a wrong | act: help their friend put together a story that does not need them in it | chance: 50
- B>G | find your friend a good solicitor, and pay for the first hour | v: benevolence | chance: 70
- R>W | tell your friend you love them, and that they must own up | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 60
- G>U | ask your friend round for a meal, and hear the whole story first | v: benevolence | chance: 70
works: The police lose interest, and {friend} rings to say it is over.
works: {friend} faces it in the end, and the friendship comes through.
fails: An officer rings {N} to ask a few more questions about Saturday night.
fails: {friend} hangs up, and does not ring again for a long, long time.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- echo: the police at the door (cycle s3)
# Every option carries title_if_fails: someone with a record (a charge that sticks), so the record is even per color.

== the police at the door
tier: echo
at: police
stages: juvenile+
age: 13-90
after: mark 'broke the law', and what was done has come to light: a witness, a camera, a friend who talked
delay: 0-3
likelier: a serious offence, a camera or a witness, a friend who was caught, a small town where people talk
rarer: a small offence long ago, nobody hurt, a big city with stretched police, a wrong owned up and settled
tone: trouble
life: conflict, home
horizon: months
roles: parent, partner, friend, the police, a solicitor
earth: two police officers on your doorstep early one morning, asking for you by name
times: Follows only some wrongs: most go unsolved, and well under 1 in 10 crimes recorded in England and Wales end in a charge (Home Office); once a witness talks, a knock is likely
scene: Early on a weekday morning there are two police officers on the doorstep, asking for {N} by name.
scene W: {N} has known, somewhere, that this day might come. There is almost a relief in it: the thing is out, and now it can be answered.
scene U: {N} counts quickly what they could know and what they cannot, and how much hangs on the next few sentences.
scene B: {N} keeps a pleasant face at the door and thinks: what have they got, who gave it to them, and what is it worth to say nothing?
scene R: {Ns} heart kicks against the ribs. For one wild second the back door, the garden fence and the open road all look like answers.
scene G: The whole house is awake behind {N}, watching from the stairs. Whatever happens now happens to all of them.
- W | let them in, and tell them the truth from the start | v: conformity, security | mark: owned up | self_control: + | title_if_fails: someone with a record | chance: 50
- U | say politely that you will answer everything with a solicitor present | v: security | title_if_fails: someone with a record | chance: 60
- B | say nothing, admit nothing, and make them prove it | v: power, security | mark: hid a wrong | title_if_fails: someone with a record | chance: 55
- R | slip out the back door and keep walking | body: light | v: self-direction | closed: law: avoiding arrest; backfire: picked up by evening, with another charge | mark: defied an authority | title_if_fails: someone with a record | chance: 40
- G | ask them in, put the kettle on, and let the family sit with you | v: tradition, benevolence | title_if_fails: someone with a record | act: ask them in, put the kettle on, and let the family sit with them | chance: 50
- W>R | sign whatever they put in front of you, just to get it over with | v: conformity | mark: gave in to pressure | self_control: - | title_if_fails: someone with a record | act: sign whatever they put in front of them, just to get it over with | chance: 50
- U>G | ask what they know, then say only what keeps the family out of it | v: security, benevolence | title_if_fails: someone with a record | chance: 40
- B>W | offer to name everyone else involved, in return for a lighter charge | v: power, security | closed: approval: informing on others; backfire: word gets round, and old friends turn away | mark: made an enemy | title_if_fails: someone with a record | chance: 45
- R>U | tell them straight what you did, and ask what happens now | v: self-direction | mark: owned up | title_if_fails: someone with a record | chance: 60
- G>B | let the family's old friend, a solicitor, do all the talking | v: security, tradition | title_if_fails: someone with a record | chance: 50
works: It ends with a warning and a signature, and the officers leave before the kettle boils.
works: The case is dropped a month later for lack of evidence.
fails: {N} is charged, and a court date arrives in the post.
fails: {N} spends the afternoon in an interview room and the evening with a solicitor.
